 The whole game, folded: one ride state, one step of it, and one frame drawn from
 it. A port of wasm/safari.zig, and the last file in the game to be ported.

 safari.zig is the module the browser sees, and most of it is an ABI -- twenty
 exports handing the blitter numbers it cannot compute. THREE THINGS IN IT ARE NOT
 ABI, and those are what this chapter is:

   THE STATE FOLD. advance() is four calls in a fixed order and a restart at the
     finish line. The order is the whole content: the truck steps against the NEW
     rider distance, because its schedule anchors to where the rider is now.
   THE FRAME. renderFrame() computes the live focal and the view yaw, then calls
     render.frame -- whose outer sequence (backdrop, ground, everything that
     stands up in depth order) had lived in the browser poc because half its
     drawers were unported. They are all ported now, so it belongs here, above
     Render rather than inside it: the dispatch has to cite every drawer, and
     TruckBody cites Render, so a dispatch inside Render would be a cycle.
   THE CAMERA PULL-IN. The lens narrows as he leans and as he watches a crossing
     cat. `cam-focal`, `focal-for-lean` and `focal-for-gaze` have been ported and
     idle since Camera landed, because nothing above them existed to compute the
     two fractions they take. This is that thing.

 WHAT STAYS OUTSIDE, and it is a short list: the history ring, the exports, and
 the mutable statics that hold the current ride. Those are the shim's, and the
 shim is where they belong -- a Codex program is pure and a ring buffer is not.
 What the shim loses by this chapter existing is all the LOGIC it used to carry:
 the restart, the step order, the focal, the view-yaw fold. It keeps a value, an
 array and twenty exported readouts.

 We say:

Section: The Live Camera

 THE LENS PULLS IN -- narrows -- on two independent things, and the frame takes
 the deeper of them. Leaning into a corner narrows it toward 0.35x; watching a
 crossing cat or gawking at the roadside pigs narrows it too. `camera.camFocal`
 is a min over the two, so whichever is more distracted wins.

 THE WHOLE FRAME READS ONE FOCAL. The projection, the backdrop and the sun all
 take it, which is what keeps them one camera; the only place the STABLE base
 focal survives is the critter size cull, and Render's own prose argues that at
 length (culling animals on a leaned focal would pop them in and out of a corner).

  cat-attention : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  cat-attention (w) (s) =
    let seg = list-at w s.segment
    in if seg.has-cat then cat-focus (seg.cat.along - s.along) s.v else 0.0

  ride-focal : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  ride-focal (w) (s) =
    let lean-frac = real-min (real-abs s.tilt / max-lean) 1.0
    in let attention = real-max (cat-attention w s) (gaze-focus s.focus)
    in cam-focal lean-frac attention

 The extra camera yaw on top of his heading: the distracted gaze toward the pigs
 plus a subtle head-turn into a lean. It swings the WHOLE view, so it folds into
 the scene pose and the backdrop heading alike.

  head-yaw-frac : Real
  head-yaw-frac = 0.15

  view-yaw-for : RiderState -> Real
  view-yaw-for (s) = s.gaze-yaw + head-yaw-frac * s.tilt

  heading-for : RiderState -> Real
  heading-for (s) = s.heading + view-yaw-for s

  pose-for : List Segment, RiderState -> Pose
  pose-for (w) (s) =
    Pose { along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw + view-yaw-for s, hw = (list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0 }

 THE BIKE'S LEAN AS THE CANVAS SHOULD ROLL IT, which is not quite the lean the
 simulation carries. The lean search leaves a residue around 1e-5 rad on a
 straight that never quite reaches zero; below a thousandth of a radian -- under
 half a pixel of bank at the screen edge -- the camera is upright for every
 practical purpose and the blitter's straight-line fast paths key on exactly
 zero. The simulation is untouched: this is a read, not a state.

  roll-deadband : Real
  roll-deadband = 0.001

 THE `let` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT SHOULD NOT BE. Written as a bare
 `if real-abs s.tilt < roll-deadband then 0.0 else s.tilt`, this function is
 INLINED and never emitted, so the shim -- which is outside the program -- fails
 to link against it. Binding the field first makes it a real function. Measured
 over four variants: the same shape over a scalar parameter emits, and the
 arithmetic-bodied `view-yaw-for` beside it emits. PORTING_NOTES B12.

  rider-roll : RiderState -> Real
  rider-roll (s) =
    let t = s.tilt
    in if real-abs t < roll-deadband then 0.0 else t

Section: Drawing What Is Sorted

 The depth order Render produced, painted far to near. All six kinds draw.

  tree-fwd-of : List TreeItem, Integer -> Real
  tree-fwd-of (ts) (i) = (list-at ts i).fwd

 How many trees stand strictly nearer than this one. Only the closest four get a
 round shaded trunk, and since a roadside pair shares one depth, "fewer than four
 strictly closer" is the nearest two pairs.

  closer-count : List TreeItem, Real, Integer -> Integer
  closer-count (ts) (f) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then 0
    else if (list-at ts i).fwd < f then 1 + closer-count ts f (i + 1)
    else closer-count ts f (i + 1)

  crown-shade-of : Real -> Real
  crown-shade-of (fwd) =
    let raw = 1.0 - fwd / crown-shade-dist
    in if raw < 0.0 then 0.0 else (if raw > 1.0 then 1.0 else raw)

  draw-one-tree : List TreeItem, Integer, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-tree (ts) (i) (cf) =
    let t = list-at ts i
    in tree-draw t.right t.fwd t.height t.color cf camera-w (closer-count ts t.fwd 0 < 4) (t.fwd < detail-dist) (crown-shade-of t.fwd)

 A tower's four base corners are re-derived from its yaw at draw time and mapped
 through the frame that owns it, which is why the collector kept the recipe rather
 than the points.

  tower-base : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TowerItem, Integer -> List RiderPt
  tower-base (w) (ch) (pose) (tw) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else [map-pt w ch pose tw.map (base-corner-ax k tw.a0 tw.x0 tw.yaw).a (base-corner-ax k tw.a0 tw.x0 tw.yaw).x]
         & tower-base w ch pose tw (k + 1)

  draw-one-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TowerItem, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-tower (w) (ch) (pose) (tw) (cf) (step) =
    draw-flat (tower-base w ch pose tw 0) (map-pt w ch pose tw.map tw.a0 tw.x0) cf camera-w (step + tw.off)

  draw-one-critter : Billboard, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-critter (b) (cf) = critter-draw b.right b.fwd b.height b.cp b.face-right cf camera-w

  draw-one-cat : CatItem, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-cat (k) (cf) = cat-draw k.right k.fwd k.height k.pose-idx k.lift cf camera-w

 THE TRUCK TAKES TWO THINGS THE COLLECTOR DOES NOT CARRY, and both come from
 outside the depth sort: whether it is braking, which is a field on the state the
 shim steps, and whether its headlights are on, which is the sunset clock -- the
 same `sun-behind-mountains` the backdrop uses, so the beams come on at the moment
 the sun leaves the crest rather than at a time of their own. Its half width is
 the width of the segment its CENTRE landed on, chain index `tk.d`, not the
 rider's own.

  draw-one-truck : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TruckAt, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-truck (w) (ch) (pose) (tk) (braking) (cf) (step) =
    truck-draw-body w ch pose tk.d tk.along ((list-at w (list-at ch tk.d)).width / 2.0) braking (sun-behind-mountains step) cf camera-w

  draw-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Item, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-item (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (it) (braking) (cf) (step) =
   when it.kind
    is KTree -> draw-one-tree c.trees it.i cf
    is KTower -> draw-one-tower w ch pose (list-at c.towers it.i) cf step
    is KRail -> rail-draw-poly (list-at c.rails it.i) cf camera-w
    is KCow -> draw-one-critter (list-at c.cows it.i) cf
    is KCat -> draw-one-cat (list-at c.cats it.i) cf
    is KTruck -> draw-one-truck w ch pose c.truck braking cf step

  draw-order : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Boolean, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-order (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (braking) (cf) (step) (i) =
    if i >= list-length c.order then []
    else (draw-item w ch pose c (list-at c.order i) braking cf step) & draw-order w ch pose c braking cf step (i + 1)

Section: The Ride

 ONE RECORD FOR THE WHOLE RIDE, because the three things advance together and a
 caller that could step one without the others would be a bug waiting. The clock
 is a plain frame count -- safari.zig's `step_clock`, which gains one per advance
 and gives one back on the way out -- and it drives the sunset, the sun and the
 tower beacons.

  Ride = record {
    rider : RiderState,
    truck : TruckState,
    clock : Real
  }

  ride-initial : Ride
  ride-initial = Ride { rider = initial-rider-state, truck = truck-initial, clock = 0.0 }

 ONE STEP, AND THE ORDER IN IT IS THE WHOLE CONTENT. The rider moves first and the
 truck steps against his NEW route distance, because the chase schedule anchors to
 where he is now; stepping it against the old distance would leave the lead one
 frame stale at exactly the moments it is tightest. The finish line restarts the
 whole ride rather than stopping, because this is a screensaver.

  ride-next : List Segment, Ride -> Ride
  ride-next (w) (r) =
    if is-finished r.rider w then ride-initial else ride-step w r

  ride-step : List Segment, Ride -> Ride
  ride-step (w) (r) =
    let nx = get-next-rider-state r.rider w
    in let dist = route-distance w nx.segment nx.along
    in Ride { rider = nx, truck = truck-next r.truck dist w (course-length w), clock = r.clock + 1.0 }

Section: The Frame

 render.zig's own sequence: the backdrop behind everything, then the floor, then
 everything that stands up, in one depth order. The live focal reaches all three.

  ride-frame : List Segment, Ride -> List DrawCmd
  ride-frame (w) (r) =
    let s = r.rider
    in let cf = ride-focal w s
    in let vy = view-yaw-for s
    in let pose = Pose { along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw + vy, hw = (list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0 }
    in let ch = build-chain w s.segment
    in (draw (s.heading + vy) (sun-set-fraction r.clock) cf camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w s.segment pose cf camera-w)
       & (draw-order w ch pose (collect w s.segment pose cf s.along s.v r.truck.pos) r.truck.braking cf r.clock 0)

 The sun's placement for this frame, which the blitter paints itself behind the
 ranges. It reads the same heading the mountains do and the same live focal the
 rest of the frame does, so it cannot drift away from what is drawn over it.

  ride-sun : List Segment, Ride -> SunPos
  ride-sun (w) (r) =
    let cf = ride-focal w r.rider
    in sun-pos (heading-for r.rider) r.clock cf camera-w
